ISOLATION (AZL): The Primordial Nucleus of Post-Perception
An Analytical Artistic Study
Introduction: From Personal Experience to Theoretical Foundation
In the history of art, personal pain rarely converges so precisely with the founding moment of a new artistic movement. However, in the case of "Isolation" (Azl), this meeting occurs with full force. Born from the womb of medical isolation during the COVID-19 pandemic, this work transcended the expression of individual suffering to become the first seed from which the entire philosophy of "Post-Perception" grew.
This work serves as the bridge between autobiography (documented in the first part of Color Box) and theoretical foundation. It is the moment where personal experience transformed into an idea, the idea into a work, and the work into the nucleus of an integrated intellectual movement.
________________________________________
I. The Context: The Birth of the Work
From a Single Pill to a Deluge of Medicine It began with small details: the discomfort of taking a single painkiller for a headache. It ended with a body cast upon an isolation bed, surrounded by mounds of drugs, searching for an "exit" that no longer existed. In that moment, two paradoxes intersected in my consciousness:
1.	Optional Solitude: The life I lived in my studio as an artist, choosing loneliness for contemplation and creation.
2.	Compulsory Isolation: The state imposed upon me as a patient, where my will was stripped away, and my body became my prison.
This intersection is what allowed "Isolation" to evolve from a work about a pandemic into a work about human existence itself. As stated in the artist’s biography: "The moment I clearly announced the birth of this direction was through the installation work ‘Isolation’ (Azl), which formed the primary seed for the crystallization of this movement."
________________________________________
II. Description: Constructing Solitude
Space as a Body "Isolation" takes the form of a complete medical isolation room. The visitor enters a closed space where the walls and floor are entirely covered with medical leaflets—those small white papers accompanying every treatment, carrying both the promises of healing and the fears of side effects. This covering constructs the space as a "diseased body."
In the heart of the room, on the wall facing the entrance, a circle 1.5 meters in diameter is formed entirely from empty medicine boxes. This circle evokes:
•	A Giant Pill: An amplification of treatment that transforms into a burden.
•	An Infinite Loop: Representation of time at a standstill.
•	An Observational Eye: A missing camera; a medical system controlling the body.
In front of this circle lies a narrow bed (190 x 50 cm), reduced to the minimal dimensions required to house a body. At its edge sits a sculptural head—eroded, fragmented, and exhausted. From the back of the head, veins and arteries hang, stuffed with thousands of actual pills—an endless flow of "cure" turning into a biological and symbolic weight.
The Presence of Absence The most potent element, however, is absence. In the suffocating silence, the visitor hears a very low heartbeat broadcast through hidden directional speakers. This pulse:
•	Defies identification; it feels as though it emanates from within the visitor.
•	Transforms the viewer from an observer into an embodiment of the work.
•	The experience is completed by the scent of surgical disinfectants permeating the air, adding an olfactory dimension to the multi-sensory experience.
________________________________________
III. Philosophical Analysis: Why is this the Nucleus?
1.	Fragile Perception: The work tests the fragility of the body’s perception in the face of pain. The recipient loses the boundary between their own body and the artwork.
2.	Truth as a Construction: The work does not offer objective truth about illness; it builds a "perceptual truth" through the accumulation of 550 boxes, 1,450 leaflets, and 920 pills. Truth here is what the recipient feels, not what merely exists.
3.	Art as a Perceptual Laboratory: It is not a painting to be seen; it is a laboratory testing the limits of one’s endurance of isolation and existential anxiety.
4.	The Recipient as a Partner, Not a Spectator: The faint pulse and the overwhelming scent ensure the work is not complete without the physical and sensory engagement of the recipient.
5.	Perceptual Immunity: The work trains the consciousness to face the ambiguity and fragility of the body—an exercise in resisting perceptual deception, even when that deception comes from within one's own flesh.
________________________________________
IV. Categorization: Testing the Universal
"Isolation" bridges two of the movement's six categories:
•	Category IV: Experimental Universality: It tests the recipient's endurance limits through a "deluge" of medical artifacts.
•	Category V: Post-Universality: It reconstructs the state of "after" medical isolation, asking: What remains after the collapse?
________________________________________
V. The Message: What Remains with the Recipient
When a visitor leaves "Isolation," they do not leave as they entered. They carry with them:
•	A New Sensory Memory: The persistent pulse, the scent of antiseptic, the white leaflets.
•	A Shifted Relationship with Medicine: Treatment is no longer just a solution, but a potential burden.
•	The Ultimate Question: What remains of a human when time, relationships, and movement are stripped away, leaving them alone to face their own body?
Conclusion: From the Nucleus to the Tree
If the Post-Perception movement today is a tree extending through 30 works, 6 books, and 6 categories, then "Isolation" is the nucleus from which it grew. It is the first laboratory of consciousness.
Ibrahim Shalabi
